<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Mauretania</h1><p class="st-meta">225 BCE – 40 CE (approx.) · capital Iol-Caesarea</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Berber kingdom of the Moors in Morocco and western Algeria, ending with Juba II's learned court at Caesarea.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauri">Mauri</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> peoples of northern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-morocco">Morocco</a>, were united under kings by the late 3rd century BCE; the first well-known king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baga-of-mauretania">Baga</a>, provided an escort to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masinissa">Masinissa</a> in 206 BCE. The kingdom lay west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moulouya-river">Mulucha</a> (Moulouya) river and included old Phoenician towns such as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lixus">Lixus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangier">Tingis</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The kings mixed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a>, Punic and later Roman and Greek culture. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bocchus-i">Bocchus I</a> handed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jugurtha">Jugurtha</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/numidia">Numidia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a> in 105 BCE, gaining western Numidia. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bocchus-ii">Bocchus II</a> bequeathed the kingdom to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, Augustus installed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juba-ii">Juba II</a> in 25 BCE, and Juba married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleopatra-selene-ii">Cleopatra Selene</a>, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleopatra-vii">Cleopatra VII</a>. Juba, a scholar, rebuilt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caesarea-mauretaniae">Iol</a> as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-caesarea">Caesarea</a>, and the kingdom exported purple dye, citrus wood and wild animals.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 40 CE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caligula">Caligula</a> summoned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juba-ii">Juba</a>'s son Ptolemy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and had him executed. After a revolt led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aedemon">Aedemon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> annexed the kingdom, dividing it in 42 CE into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mauretania-tingitana">Mauretania Tingitana</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mauretania-caesariensis">Mauretania Caesariensis</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-mauretania.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Mauretania" width="320" height="126"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mauretania_et_Numidia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>225 BCE – 40 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Iol-Caesarea</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Bocchus I, Juba II, Ptolemy of Mauretania</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Berber religion, Punic religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Berber, Punic, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/morocco">Morocco</a>, <a href="/countries/algeria">Algeria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Mauretania, Maurusia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-92&amp;focus=kingdom-of-mauretania" aria-label="Kingdom of Mauretania on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4510 1011 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Mauretania lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4830 1267L4845 1161M4830 1267L5304 942M4830 1267L4854 1142M4830 1267L5062 1084"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="